      "en": {
        "title": "Which pair of readings belongs to which drop?",
        "prompt": "Make a short measurement log for three separate drops. Choose one route.\n\nYour own measurements. Drop a small, soft ball onto a clear floor from three different heights below your shoulder. Read the height with a ruler. You can time each fall using a side-on video with a known frame rate. For each drop, note the frame at release and the frame at first contact with the floor.\n\nPrague lab notes. For steel ball 1 the lab record contains three separate drops: the measured time was 0.285 s at 40 cm, 201 ms at 20 cm and 0.349 s at 60 cm. These measurements come from experiment 2 at the Czech Technical University in Prague on 10 March 2017. The height scale had a resolution of 1 cm and the timer a resolution of 1 ms.\n\nLabel both columns of your table with the quantity and unit. Pair the drop height and fall time for each drop, using one unit consistently within each column. State where the measurements came from. Explain the two positions you use for the height and the two events that start and end the fall time. Keep your log and any recordings you made for the later analysis.",
        "hint": "Keep the two readings for each drop together when converting units. In a video, the difference between the frame numbers gives the number of frame intervals that have passed. How long is one interval at your frame rate?",
        "solution": "For the Prague record, the rows under drop height in cm and fall time in ms are 20 | 201, 40 | 285 and 60 | 349. The rows can be in any order. Each time belongs to the drop from the corresponding height. One valid height reference is the vertical distance from the bottom of the ball at release to the floor. Time runs from release to first contact with the floor. For your own video, divide the difference between the frame numbers by the frame rate to get the fall time. Report your own readings and their source.",
        "evidenceCriteria": [
